% cryptarithmetic
%
% The Logical English (for LPS) twin of an Epilog game, written by the Epilog translator
% from cryptarithmetic.hrf (kept in sources/): the operations are causal laws, the
% views over the state intensional fluents.
% The scenario is a play EpilogJS made; LPS2's final state of it is checked against EpilogJS's
% (test_epilog.pl).
% Source: the Epilog Games page, http://epilog.stanford.edu/homepage/game.php?game=cryptarithmetic
%
% DISCLAIMER. This program was written by a translator from a program of another
% system. It is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind, express or
% implied, including any warranty that it is accurate, complete or fit for a
% particular purpose. The translation may be wrong: check it against its source
% before relying on it. It is not legal, tax, insurance, financial or other
% professional advice. Its authors accept no liability for any loss or damage
% arising from its use.

the target language is: lps.

the maximum time is 10.

the actions are:
    the move set with *a letter* and *a digit* is made; known as set.

the fluents are:
    *a thing* is a control; known as control.
    *a letter* is a step; known as step.
    *a letter* is the value of *a digit*; known as value.
    the goal of *a role* is *a number*; known as goal.
    the move set with *a letter* and *a digit* is legal; known as legal_set.
    the game is terminal; known as terminal.

the templates are:
    *a digit* is a digit.
    *a thing* is the succ of *a second thing*.

the knowledge base cryptarithmetic includes:

the text of the game is at "sources/cryptarithmetic.hrf".

1 is a digit.

2 is a digit.

3 is a digit.

4 is a digit.

5 is a digit.

6 is a digit.

7 is a digit.

8 is a digit.

9 is a digit.

0 is a digit.

s is the succ of e.

e is the succ of n.

n is the succ of d.

d is the succ of m.

m is the succ of o.

o is the succ of r.

r is the succ of y.

y is the succ of z.

the move set with a letter and a digit is legal at a time if
    the letter is a step at the time
    and the digit is a digit.

the goal of robot is 100 at a time if
    s is the value of 9 at the time
    and e is the value of 5 at the time
    and n is the value of 6 at the time
    and d is the value of 7 at the time
    and m is the value of 1 at the time
    and o is the value of 0 at the time
    and r is the value of 8 at the time
    and y is the value of 2 at the time.

the goal of robot is 0 at a time if
    it is not the case that s is the value of 9 at the time.

the goal of robot is 0 at a time if
    it is not the case that e is the value of 5 at the time.

the goal of robot is 0 at a time if
    it is not the case that n is the value of 6 at the time.

the goal of robot is 0 at a time if
    it is not the case that d is the value of 7 at the time.

the goal of robot is 0 at a time if
    it is not the case that m is the value of 1 at the time.

the goal of robot is 0 at a time if
    it is not the case that o is the value of 0 at the time.

the goal of robot is 0 at a time if
    it is not the case that r is the value of 8 at the time.

the goal of robot is 0 at a time if
    it is not the case that y is the value of 2 at the time.

the game is terminal at a time if
    z is a step at the time.

when the move set with a letter and a digit is made
then the letter is the value of the digit.

when the move set with a letter and a digit is made
    and the letter is a step
    and the letter is the succ of a thing
then the thing is a step
    and it is not the case that the letter is a step.

it must not be true that
    the move set with a letter and a digit is made
    and it is not the case that the move set with the letter and the digit is legal.

initially s is a step
    and robot is a control.

% EpilogJS's state after the play (the twin's final state under LPS2 must be the same):
%   - control(robot)
%   - step(z)
%   - value(d,1)
%   - value(e,7)
%   - value(m,8)
%   - value(n,4)
%   - value(o,5)
%   - value(r,2)
%   - value(s,0)
%   - value(y,9)
scenario play is:
    the move set with s and 0 is made from 1 to 2.
    the move set with e and 7 is made from 2 to 3.
    the move set with n and 4 is made from 3 to 4.
    the move set with d and 1 is made from 4 to 5.
    the move set with m and 8 is made from 5 to 6.
    the move set with o and 5 is made from 6 to 7.
    the move set with r and 2 is made from 7 to 8.
    the move set with y and 9 is made from 8 to 9.

